[
  "Welcome back to The Sound Above. I am Mike Hall. Today we enter Philosophy, Empathy & Craftsmanship. Before code synthesis models and automated agents, developers met face to face to establish standards of craftsmanship. Today we rewatch Episode 18: Hadi Hariri from SCNA 2012 (2012).",
  "Hadi represented JetBrains at Chicago conferences, arguing for ergonomic IDEs that empower developers without arrogance.",
  "Who showed Hadi Hariri that software engineers are not special snowflakes? Why does empathy for real-world workers matter in systems design? What principles guided Hadi Hariri when this conversation was recorded? How do those lessons inform the software systems we design today?",
  "Roll archive footage (YouTube ID: pOl23zYt_Nw). Watch for the unvarnished hallway exchanges, the emphasis on craft over speed, and the sheer enthusiasm of engineers taking responsibility for their software.",
  "Software engineers must remember they are toolsmiths serving human needs, especially as AI generates the boilerplate. When implementation is cheap, verification and clarity become everything. We cannot accept the mirage of competence. Let us examine the terminal contracts directly.",
  "Thank you for joining today's session. Full transcripts, provenance notes, and companion research are available at just3ws.com. In our next broadcast, we explore Episode 19. Until next time, keep your feedback loops fast and your code humane."
]